# REQUIRED CRONTAB LINES -- now EMPTY, and that is the finished state, not a wipe.
#
# cron.service on this box died on 2026-08-20 20:48:44 UTC with Result: oom-kill (3.1G peak on
# a 4GB box) and cannot be restarted without root, which user `quant` does not have by design.
# Every line this file used to demand was therefore inert for 8 days while the guard that reads
# it went on merging them back and reporting success -- evidence of protection that did not
# exist. All seven have been migrated to systemd USER timers, which is the desk's real
# scheduler (75 live timers) and the sanctioned non-root path:
#
#   hourly_controller.py        -> quant-hourly-controller (ops/hourly-controller.timer)
#   check_research_health.py    -> quant-research-health
#   check_bar_span.py           -> quant-bar-span         (added 2026-08-28)
#   auto_push.sh                -> quant-auto-push        (added 2026-08-28)
#   check_unit_health.py        -> quant-unit-health      (added 2026-08-28)
#   refresh_cot_zcache.py       -> quant-cot-zcache       (added 2026-08-28)
#   check_governance_pulse.py   -> quant-governance-pulse (added 2026-08-28; was never in this
#                                  file, which is part of why it went 8 days unnoticed)
#
# The unit files are committed under ops/ and their live-vs-committed drift is fenced by
# scripts/check_unit_parity.py -- a stronger check than this file ever was, because it compares
# against what systemd is ACTUALLY running rather than against a text file nothing executes.
#
# DO NOT re-add lines here to "be safe". A demanded line in a dead crontab reads as a fence and
# is not one; that false green is the entire lesson above. If a new organ needs a schedule, it
# gets a user timer with a committed copy in ops/.
